\f0\b Final Clarifications \'97 Round 4 (Quick)\

\f1\b0 Just three small items before I lock the spec:\
21. Input format:\uc0\u8232 Most delay plugins accept mono or stereo input. Should OpenSpatialDelay accept both? (I'd recommend yes \'97 mono input is standard for spatial workflows since you're spatializing a point source, but stereo input should be gracefully handled too.) For v1 we can receive a mono source, but later I would expect to be able to also send a stereo source into it, which would then inform the way the delays behave as it should then process the Left and Right channels separately.\
22. Cumulative pitch shift behavior:\uc0\u8232 You said "each delay is increased or decreased by the set semitone amount." To confirm \'97 does this mean\'a0cumulative per tap?\
Example at +2 semitones: Tap 1 = +2st, Tap 2 = +4st, Tap 3 = +6st, etc.\
Or\'a0uniform\'a0\'97 every tap gets the same fixed pitch shift?\
The cumulative approach is more creative and is what most spatial delay plugins do (it creates that classic "rising/falling into the distance" effect). Just want to confirm. Great question, cumulative is the right approach. \
23. Distance attenuation:\uc0\u8232 When a tap is positioned farther from the listener on the 2D map, should it be\'a0quieter\'a0(physically accurate \'97 inverse distance law) and have\'a0more high-frequency rolloff\'a0(air absorption simulation)? Or should all taps be equal loudness regardless of visual position?\
My recommendation: yes to distance attenuation, it's what makes the spatial image convincing. But it should have a\'a0"Distance Law" knob\'a0(0\'96100%) so the user can dial it back to taste. Excellent question. I think for v1 let\'92s avoid the distance knob, but later we can add a distance knob per tap and we will define exactly what that means when we get there. \
\
By the way, after we conclude the interview, please provide me with a full report of the project, including a logical roadmap based on the various features we have discussed adding in later version. \
